From 92633bbd853acd8ccc059274cc4055fcdef23f0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:38:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 49/50] feat(text): a text layer's script runs like any other
 property script

A text layer's script ran in a runtime of its own: a closure with a
stand-in thisLayer holding only `text`, an engine with frametime and
time, and none of the rest. 176 scripts in 22 wallpapers drive a text
this way (clocks, dates, now-playing titles), and in that runtime:

- applyUserProperties () never ran, so a date drew its month as
  "undefined" and ignored the separator and names the user set (13
  scripts, 8 wallpapers);
- `shared` (39 scripts) and the media events (20 scripts) were absent,
  and so were engine.userProperties, canvasSize, the timers and the
  audio buffers;
- thisLayer had no other property and no methods.

The text is now a property like origin or alpha: its script is queued
with the rest, sees the real thisLayer, thisScene and engine, gets init,
applyUserProperties, update and the media events in Wallpaper Engine's
order, and the layer draws what the property holds. A write to the
text from another layer's script reaches the layer too. The separate
runtime is gone.

engine.registerAsset () exists: it marks a file the editor packs with the
wallpaper, and returns the path. A day-name script calls it fifteen times
at load and stopped at the first.

